Murray,
Thank you very much for your response and help. That suggestion did indeed fix the error message I was getting. However, it brings up a new issue that I haven't been able to troubleshoot.

The reason the sessioncov input file had NAs present was because we did not trap at those 2 grids for the first 2 sessions (or 6 occasions). So, to account for a difference in usage, I added a usage column to the detector/trap input file. When I read the new trap input file into secr and call up the usage(), it seems to be reading the usage column correctly:
